Quoting James Anderson <anders...@netscape.net>:

> > Hello,
> >
> > I have completed a summer of bird surveying in a very fragmented

> > combination of forest and grassland. I didn't stratify for various reas=
ons

> > and decided to wait and post-stratify or incorporate covariates if

> > possible.  It turns out the key species I=92m working with were all sig=
hted

> > within the forest patches.  I=92ve considered breaking up transects and=
 this

> > doesn=92t seem possible because the fragmentation is too great.  For

> > example, I frequently recorded individuals while walking transects thro=
ugh

> > grassland while I observed them in forest patches.  Almost all transect=
s

> > are in an irregular, fragmented combination of grassland and forest.  I

> > have cover estimates for grassland versus forest for the area searched =
for

> > each transect, and I recorded the points along each transect where ther=
e

> > was a change from forest to grassland anticipating this might help for

> > stratification or developing covariates after the survey was completed.

> > It seems covariates should work somehow but I'm new to this. I also hav=
e

> > an estimate of forested and grassland area for the entire study area

> > extrapolated from my cover estimates at transects. I=92m looking for ad=
vice

> > on how to make the density estimates better in this situation.  Please

> > reply to James at anders...@netscape.net.  Thank you.
> >
> > Also I was thinking (perhaps not too clearly) that maybe because all th=
e

> > sightings are in the forest this simplifies things a bit and I can stil=
l

> > get a reliable abundance estimate without considering different strata.=
  I

> > just analyze grassland and forest together and I can report the abundan=
ce

> > estimate as being for the forested area only.  It=92s just the density =
that

> > would be misleading because the birds are actually concentrated within =
the

> > forest not spread out over all strata.  Is this correct or wrong?

James, yes, that is the best way to tackle this.  As you had no birds in =
the
grassland habitat, you can also easily get a density estimate for forest =
by
dividing your abundance estimate by the area of forest within your study =
area.

Steve Buckland

