From u5900eadecec2d82d at groups.google.com  Thu Jan 24 17:13:38 2008
From: u5900eadecec2d82d at groups.google.com (Distance Development)
Date: Thu, 24 Jan 2008 17:13:38 +0000
Subject: Re: error in bootstrapping
Message-ID: <6AyIPkARpKM.hItRNlIJO18J@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling/c/6AyIPkARpKM/m/hItRNlIJO18J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Marzia and Caterina,

I'm cc'ing my reply to the distance-sampling list, as it might be useful
to others who get the same problem.

Marzia Marcheselli wrote:
> > Dear Authors
> > we are dealing with data on point transect surveys to be analysed using
> > Distance 5 (CDS engine).
> > In performing bootstrap variance estimation (both resampling samples -
> > which is our primary focus - and observations within samples), the
> > program crashed.
> > As suggested in the pdf reference manual, the project file, the error
> > message file and the original data are contained in the attachment.
> > It is worth noting that this in not the only dataset giving rise to the
> > crash with the same error message.
> > Thank you very much for your interest, we are looking forward to hear
> > from you.
> > Best regards
> > Marzia Marcheselli and Caterina Pisani

The basic problem is that you are doing a bootstrap fitting 3 different
detection functions based on only 33 observations.  While this is
perhaps a sensible thing to do in general, to quantify the model
selection uncertainty in selecting among plausible detection function
models, it is going to be a bit tricky when you have relatively few
observations, especially for point transect data as you have here.  In
general, we recommend getting around 90+ samples for a robust analysis
of point transect data -- although clearly it depends on how wide the
true detection function shoulder is, and whether there are any other
problems (violations of assumptions, etc).  So, you should take all
analyses you do with such a small sample with a pinch of salt.

When you do a bootstrap resample with so few observations, many of the
bootstrap samples will have, by chance, almost no observations at all in
them.  So the results will be quite unreliable - and you might end up
with unreasonably wide confidence intervals.  Also, Distance sometimes
does not work with very few observations, so it might crash -- although
it shouldn't.  Actually in this case, after some debugging, I found that
it's crashing on a bootstrap dataset that has 46 observations in it, so
this isn't the problem here.

Despite my reservations about your small sample size, the half-normal
detection function fit to your original data looks quite nice and well
behaved, with a reasonably wide shoulder and smooth decline in estimated
detectability and good fit to the data.  One possible problem is that
you don't do any truncation.  So Distance truncates at the largest
observation distance, which in your case is 0.11.  However, the
estimated detection probability at this distance in the original data is
almost zero.  Instead it's better to truncate so that the estimated
detection probability at the largest distance is rather higher -- tends
to lead to more reliable estimation in general (see Intro to Distance
Sampling book, section 1.5.3 for example) and probably more reliable in
the Distance software too.  I tried truncating at 0.08, which leaves 31
observations but a more reasonable estimated detection probability at
this distance of about 0.1.  Perhaps a little more truncation might work
too, but I didn't want to further thin your few data!  Anyway, the
estimated density was quite similar (239 vs 229), but now the bootstrap
runs fine.

You may want to stop reading at this point, but below are two more
esoteric fixes.

I had a more detailed look at where the error was occurring.  From the
output in the log file, I saw that the program crashes during the
bootstrap with the following message:

-- Start of Console Error Stream --
forrtl: error (65): floating invalid

Image              PC        Routine            Line        Source

MCDS.exe           004E604F  Unknown               Unknown  Unknown
MCDS.exe           004E0708  Unknown               Unknown  Unknown
MCDS.exe           004DCA02  Unknown               Unknown  Unknown
MCDS.exe           004DA8D6  Unknown               Unknown  Unknown
MCDS.exe           004D8FBF  Unknown               Unknown  Unknown
MCDS.exe           00424656  PDFIT                     319  Cpdfit.for
MCDS.exe           00450AB9  FXEST                     123  F0est.for
MCDS.exe           0045F1A5  FMOD                      219  Fmod.for
MCDS.exe           0045E2B9  F0MOD                      44  Fmod.for
MCDS.exe           004476CC  ESTPARM                   487  Estmte.for
MCDS.exe           0044562F  ESTMTE                    163  Estmte.for
MCDS.exe           0040158D  BOOTSTRAP                  87  Boot.for
MCDS.exe           004452D9  ESTPROC                    96  Estmte.for
MCDS.exe           0041370A  CNTRL                     113  Control.for
MCDS.exe           00438245  DISTANCE                  267  Distance.for
MCDS.exe           004F2539  Unknown               Unknown  Unknown
MCDS.exe           004C8CC3  Unknown               Unknown  Unknown
kernel32.dll       7C816FD7  Unknown               Unknown  Unknown

I had a look through the code, and line 319 of Cpdfit.for is the line
where the external optimization routine is called - the one that tries
to find the maximum likelihood solution given some starting values.
This is not a part of the Distance code, so I couldn't investigate what
inside the opimization was going wrong, but it's likely a numerical
overflow as it tries a silly value for a detection function parameter.
So, another solution is to put constraints on the detection function
parameters, so they can't do this.

To achieve this, I did the following.  You had 3 models set up: half
normal+cosine; half normal+simple polynomial; half normal+hermite
polynomial.  I see from the analysis of the original data that the
fitted value for the half normal scale parameter (listed as A(1) in the
output) was 0.0347 with a standard error of 0.0029.  For a new Model
Definition, in the Detection Function | Constraints tab I set up
constraints on this 1 key function parameter for all 3 models where it
was between 0.002 and 0.4 - this seems like a reasonably wide range so
it shouldn't bias the estimates, but will keep it from numerical
problems.  I then re-ran the bootstrap and it ran fine.

A third solution might be to use good starting values for the
optimizations.  I tried this as follows.  You allowed it to choose
adjustment terms itself.  I think, with such a small dataset, fixing it
at a maximum of one adjustment term  is fine -- particularly when there
is such strong evidence in the original dataset than no adjustments are
needed with the half normal detection function.  So, I set up 4 models
in a new analysis: half normal with manual selection of 0 adjustments;
half normal with manual selection of 1 cosine term order 2; half normal
with manual selection of 1 simple polynomial of order 4; half normal
with manual selection of 1 Hermite polynomial of order 4.  I then put
starting values for all these parameters based on the maximum likelihood
estimates found for that model on the original dataset.  Re-running the
bootstrap, I again had no problems.

Hope this helps,

  - Len

