From u5900eadecec2d82d at groups.google.com  Wed Jan 30 22:21:11 2008
From: u5900eadecec2d82d at groups.google.com (Distance Development)
Date: Wed, 30 Jan 2008 22:21:11 +0000
Subject: Re: Disappearing factor levels of covariates
Message-ID: <RJhoS6g7BeQ.ssqEX5iuvXkJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <RJhoS6g7BeQ.IWaeCrrQ6q4J@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
References: <RJhoS6g7BeQ.IWaeCrrQ6q4J@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling/c/RJhoS6g7BeQ/m/ssqEX5iuvXkJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Florian,

The last factor corresponds with the intercept (parameter A(1)) - i.e.,
it is the reference level, as you suspected.  This is explained in the
online manual -- type Factor in the index, and look at the topic listed
under "Factor and Non-factor covariates in MCDS".  RTFM!

There are many ways to turn factor levels into a design matrix, and our
choice in Distance to use the last one as a "reference" is arbitrary.

Cheers,

  - len

